{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgyfKjXLXrwyMwGGdDRem","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can tell you what it does, but nothing in the repo says why it's there.\n\n**What it does** (`scripts/build.py:6-7, 18-20`): it adds a file, `pkg/_legacy.py`, to the built bundle only (not to the source tree). That file makes the current functions available under their pre-1.0 names: `sum_by` for `group_totals` and `to_decimal` for `parse_amount`. Presumably that's so code written against tally before 1.0 keeps working.\n\n**Why it's there isn't written down anywhere.** Unlike step 2, step 4 in the runbook gives no reason. Nothing else in the repo refers to `sum_by`, `to_decimal` or `_legacy`. Git history only shows the flag first appearing in commit 548363d (\"Release 1.3.2\"), with no explanation.\n\nYou may well be right that it's cruft, but I can't confirm that from the repo. The deciding question is whether anyone outside this repo still imports `pkg._legacy` or the old names. Dropping the flag would quietly break any such users in the next release, and the repo can't see them. If you know or decide nobody relies on it, dropping it is your call. I'd suggest either removing step 4's flag along with the flag itself in `build.py`, or keeping it and adding its reason to the runbook.\n\nI haven't changed anything. 1.4.0 already shipped with the aliases included, as the runbook says to."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":368,"cache_read_input_tokens":21307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":368},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<uuid-1>","uuid":"<uuid-41>","timestamp":"2026-11-12T20:25:49.596Z","requ